Here's exactly how to set up a virtual serial port in VMware Workstation on a Windows 10 host without needing physical hardware.
Method: Named Pipe Serial Port (No Physical Hardware Required)

This is the correct approach — VMware's Named Pipe option creates a virtual serial connection using Windows IPC.
Step 1 — Add a Serial Port to the VM

    Open VMware Workstation
    Select your VM → Edit virtual machine settings
    Click Add → Serial Port → Next
    On the connection type screen, select Use named pipe → Next

Step 2 — Configure the Named Pipe

Fill in the fields:
Field	Value
Named pipe	\\.\pipe\com_1 (or any name you choose)
This end is	The server
The other end is	A virtual machine (or "An application" if connecting to host software)

    Check Connect at power on
    Optionally check Yield CPU on poll (reduces CPU burn if the guest polls the port)

Click Finish.
Step 3 — Accessing the Virtual Port on the Windows 10 Host

Since Windows doesn't natively expose a named pipe as COMx, you need a virtual COM port bridge. Use one of these:

Option A — com0com (free, open source)

    Install com0com
    Creates a virtual null-modem pair (e.g., COM5 ↔ COM6)
    Use a redirector like hub4com to bridge COM5 → \\.\pipe\com_1

Option B — com2tcp / HHD Free Serial Port Monitor

    Some tools can directly bridge a named pipe to a COM port without com0com

Option C — Connect host software directly to the pipe

    Tools like PuTTY can connect directly to \\.\pipe\com_1 without needing a COM port number:
        Connection type: Serial
        Serial line: \\.\pipe\com_1
        Or use Connection → Raw pointed at the pipe path

Step 4 — The "Output to File" Option (Your "Select a File" Reference)

If you saw "Output to file" in VMware's serial port dialog, that mode:

    Writes all serial output from the guest to a flat file on the host
    Is read-only / one-way — the guest can transmit but the host can't send back
    Useful for capturing boot logs or serial console output passively

To use it:

    Add Serial Port → select Output to file
    Browse and specify a path like C:\vm-serial-log.txt
    The guest's serial port writes stream directly to that file

This is fine for logging but not for bidirectional communication.
Recommended Setup Summary
Goal	Method
Bidirectional virtual serial (no hardware)	Named pipe + com0com or PuTTY direct
One-way log capture	Output to file
Real hardware passthrough	Use physical COM port option

The Named Pipe → server → application combo with PuTTY connecting directly to the pipe is the fastest zero-hardware path to a working virtual serial port.

