Good question — this trips people up because VMware's named pipe serial port has a specific flag that must be enabled for the host Windows side to see and negotiate the port properly.
The Critical Toggle: "Yield CPU on Poll"

This is not the one. Don't confuse it with the real required flag below.
What Must Actually Be Enabled
✅ "Connect at power on"

Must be checked or the pipe never instantiates — Windows has nothing to connect to.
✅ "The other end is: An application" (not "A virtual machine")

If you set the other end to "A virtual machine", VMware expects another VM on the other end of the pipe, not a host process. For host-side tools (PuTTY, com0com, your app) you must set it to "An application".
Baud / Data Bits / Stop Bits / Parity / Flow Control

Here is the hard truth about VMware named pipe serial:

    VMware does not enforce or negotiate baud rate, data bits, stop bits, or parity on named pipe virtual serial ports. The pipe is a raw byte stream — these parameters are ignored at the hypervisor level.

What this means practically:
Parameter	VMware Named Pipe Behavior
Baud rate	Ignored — pipe runs at memory speed
Data bits	Ignored
Stop bits	Ignored
Parity	Ignored
XON/XOFF	Must be handled by guest OS driver
RTS/CTS	Not forwarded — VMware does not emulate modem control lines
DSR/DTR	Not forwarded — same reason
DCD/RI	Not available

So if your guest software requires hardware flow control (RTS/CTS, DSR/DTR), it will either hang waiting for a signal that never comes, or you must configure the guest's COM driver to ignore modem control lines.
Making Windows Host Recognize the Port

Since a named pipe is not a real COM port, Windows Device Manager will never show it on its own. You need one of these:
Option A — com0com + hub4com (full COM emulation)

    Install com0com — creates a virtual null modem pair, e.g. COM5 ↔ COM6
    In com0com setup, for the port you'll use, enable these flags:
        ✅ cts=on — holds CTS high permanently (unblocks RTS/CTS flow control)
        ✅ dsr=on — holds DSR high (unblocks DSR/DTR handshake)
        ✅ dcd=on — holds DCD high (prevents "no carrier" hangs)
        ✅ ri=on — optional, ring indicator
    Run hub4com to bridge the pipe to the COM port:

hub4com --route=0:1 \\.\pipe\com_1 \\.\CNCB0

This gives you a real COMx in Device Manager with all control lines asserted.
Option B — Configure your app to ignore flow control

In PuTTY or whatever host app:

    Flow control → set to None
    This bypasses the missing RTS/CTS/DSR/DTR entirely

Full Checklist for It to Work

VMware Serial Port Settings:
  [✅] Connect at power on
  [✅] Named pipe: \\.\pipe\com_1
  [✅] This end is: The server
  [✅] The other end is: An application   ← most common wrong setting
  [✅] Yield CPU on poll                  ← check this to prevent CPU spin

Guest OS (Linux example):
  - /dev/ttyS0 or ttyS1
  - stty -F /dev/ttyS0 raw -crtscts       ← disable hardware flow control
  - or in minicom: Hardware flow = No

Host Windows Side:
  - com0com with cts/dsr/dcd forced ON
  - OR direct pipe access with flow control disabled in your app

The One Setting People Miss Most

"The other end is: An application" — if this is set to "A virtual machine" your host app will never connect. That single toggle is responsible for the majority of "Windows doesn't see the port" issues with VMware named pipe serial.

To run code, enable code execution and file creation in Settings > Capabilities.

