Good progress — pipe is connecting. The hollow cursor means PuTTY connected to the pipe successfully but received zero bytes. Two things need to happen:
Problem 1: Guest Isn't Sending Anything to the Serial Port

The VM has a virtual serial port (COM1 or /dev/ttyS0) but nothing is writing to it. You need to either:
If Guest is Windows:

Enable the Emergency Management Services (EMS) / SAC console — this makes Windows boot output go to COM1 automatically.

Open an elevated CMD inside the guest and run:
cmd

bcdedit /ems on
bcdedit /emssettings EMSPORT:1 EMSBAUDRATE:115200
bcdedit /bootems on
shutdown /r /t 0

Then in PuTTY set speed to 115200. On reboot you'll get the SAC> prompt in PuTTY.
If Guest is Linux:

Add a kernel console parameter. Edit /etc/default/grub in the guest:
bash

GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="console=tty0 console=ttyS0,115200n8"

Then run:
bash

sudo grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg
# or on Ubuntu:
sudo update-grub

Reboot — you'll get full kernel boot messages and a login prompt in PuTTY.
Problem 2: PuTTY Must Be Open BEFORE VM Boots

Because VMware set the pipe as server and PuTTY is the client application, the sequence matters:

Correct order:
  1. Launch PuTTY → connect to \\.\pipe\com_1  ← must be first
  2. Then boot the VM

Wrong order:
  1. Boot VM first → pipe exists but nobody is listening
  2. PuTTY connects late → misses early boot output

If you boot VM first, you miss everything. PuTTY needs to be sitting on the pipe waiting when the VM sends its first byte.
PuTTY Settings Checklist

Make sure PuTTY is configured exactly like this:
Setting	Value
Connection type	Serial
Serial line	\\.\pipe\com_1
Speed	115200
Data bits	8
Stop bits	1
Parity	None
Flow control	None

Save it as a session so you can reopen it fast on every reboot.
