Two separate problems here, both solvable. Let me address each directly.
Problem 1: GRUB Console Stalling at device-mapper / LUKS

The console=ttyS0 kernel parameter breaks the encrypted boot because the cryptsetup password prompt (sda3_crypt) gets redirected to ttyS0 — which PuTTY isn't ready to receive yet. So it just hangs waiting for a password that nobody can type.

Fix: Don't put console=ttyS0 in GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX. Instead use:
bash

GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="console=tty0 console=ttyS0,115200n8"

The console=tty0 must come first — this keeps the LUKS prompt on the physical display while ttyS0 gets a copy of output after unlock. But honestly even this can be tricky with LUKS.

Safer approach for encrypted Kali: Skip the grub console entirely. Just use getty post-boot. You don't need boot messages — you need a login shell. The grub change is optional.

Revert /etc/default/grub to stock and run update-grub to get your clean boot back.
Problem 2: The Chicken-and-Egg (PuTTY / Pipe Timing)

This is a real VMware named pipe limitation and the fix is a pipe mode swap.
The Real Fix: Flip Who is Server vs Client

Right now VMware is server, PuTTY is client — so PuTTY must connect first or it misses the pipe instantiation.

Swap it:

In VMware serial port settings:

This end is:    The server      ← change to: The client
The other end:  An application  ← keep this

Now VMware is the client — it will keep retrying the pipe connection on its own after boot. PuTTY becomes the server and can be opened anytime, before or after VM boot, and VMware will connect to it when ready.

PuTTY side — you can't use PuTTY as a pipe server directly. Use this instead:
Use pipelist + a persistent listener

The cleanest host-side solution without com0com:

Option A — Use plink as persistent server (comes with PuTTY install): Not straightforward for named pipes.

Option B — Flip to "Output to file" temporarily to verify, then use com0com properly.

Option C — The actual right answer: use a null modem approach

Run this in PowerShell on the Windows host before booting the VM — it holds the pipe open permanently:
powershell

# Creates a persistent named pipe server that stays open
$pipe = New-Object System.IO.Pipes.NamedPipeServerStream('com_1', 
    [System.IO.Pipes.PipeDirection]::InOut, 1,
    [System.IO.Pipes.PipeTransmissionMode]::Byte,
    [System.IO.Pipes.PipeOptions]::None)
Write-Host "Waiting for VM..."
$pipe.WaitForConnection()
Write-Host "VM connected"
# Then pipe to PuTTY or read/write directly

Option D — Simplest working solution right now:

Create a .bat file on the Windows host desktop:
bat

@echo off
start "" "C:\Program Files\PuTTY\putty.exe" -serial \\.\pipe\com_1 -sercfg 115200,8,n,1,N
timeout /t 3
start "" vmrun start "C:\path\to\your\vm.vmx"

This opens PuTTY first, waits 3 seconds, then starts the VM — solving the timing problem permanently with one double-click.
Clean Summary
Problem	Fix
LUKS stall	Revert grub, don't use console=ttyS0, use getty only
Chicken/egg	Bat file: PuTTY first, then vmrun starts VM after 3s delay
getty still needed	systemctl enable --now serial-getty@ttyS0.service in Kali

The .bat launcher is your immediate unblock. Get that working first, then we can revisit boot console if you still want it.

